<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2">
  <Document>
    <name>🌫️ LIVE FOG RISK/ACTIVE FOG</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> Oct 6, 2026 10:00PM EDT<br/><b>Next update:</b> Oct 6, 2026 11:00PM EDT</p><h2>LIVE FOG RISK/ACTIVE FOG</h2><p><b>Status:</b> LIVE / CURRENT MODEL (derived HRRR analysis), hourly cycles</p><p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/fog/legend.png?v=hrrr-hrrr.20261007-t01z-fog-anl-g3" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Fog index 0-100 (DERIVED from HRRR analysis, fixed absolute scale): risk owns the first 35% — <b>LOWEST 0 No risk</b> green &rarr; low fog risk &rarr; high-risk orange at 35 — then active fog by severity: light fog &rarr; moderate fog &rarr; dense fog &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 100 Extreme fog</b> dark purple. The key steps evenly (0/20/40/60/80/100) with severity written under each value, walking the fog-risk-to-active-fog path left to right. Same index always shows the same color. Thermodynamic risk with an observed-visibility density confirmation — not a copy of the visibility layer.</p><p><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NCEP HRRR 3 km analysis, hourly cycles: TMP+DPT+RH (2 m) + UGRD/VGRD (10 m) + VIS surface (derived fog-risk index with active-fog visibility gate)<br/><a href="https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl">https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl</a></p><p>Transparent outside valid water data so existing project layers (shipwrecks, lighthouses, harbors, parks) stay visible. Turn on/off independently of all other layers.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
    <Folder>
      <name>LIVE FOG RISK/ACTIVE FOG</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> Oct 6, 2026 10:00PM EDT<br/><b>Next update:</b> Oct 6, 2026 11:00PM EDT</p><h2>LIVE FOG RISK/ACTIVE FOG</h2><p>A derived fog-risk index from operational HRRR analysis: low greens mark clear air, yellows and oranges mark elevated fog risk from saturated calm air, and reds into deep purple mark active fog confirmed by collapsed visibility. Built for Great Lakes navigation, shoreline conditions, aviation awareness, and nighttime environmental monitoring. Derivation: v2: risk r = rh_factor*spread_factor*calm_factor in [0,1] (rh_factor=clip((RH-70)/30,0,1); spread_factor=clip((3-S)/3,0,1), S=TMP-DPT C deg; calm_factor=clip((6-wspd)/6,0.35,1)) -> risk zone 35*r. Corroborated density d = clip((5000-VIS)/5000,0,1)^0.8 only where VIS<5000m & RH>=90 -> fog zone 35+65*d. Index = max of the two (continuous at VIS=5000m). Risk owns 0-35, active fog by density owns 35-100. Distinct from raw visibility; dry-air speckles can never paint active fog.</p><p>Fog science (boundary-layer meteorology and aerosol microphysics) studies saturation, radiative cooling, and droplet nucleation that reduce visibility below one kilometer. Radiation fog forms over land and calm water on clear nights; advection and steam fog form where air-water temperature contrasts drive lake-effect events. The index separates fog likelihood from raw visibility so developing risk reads before, and active fog reads during, an event.</p><p><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/fog/legend.png?v=hrrr-hrrr.20261007-t01z-fog-anl-g3" width="600" alt="key"><br>Fog index 0-100 (DERIVED from HRRR analysis, fixed absolute scale): risk owns the first 35% — <b>LOWEST 0 No risk</b> green &rarr; low fog risk &rarr; high-risk orange at 35 — then active fog by severity: light fog &rarr; moderate fog &rarr; dense fog &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 100 Extreme fog</b> dark purple. The key steps evenly (0/20/40/60/80/100) with severity written under each value, walking the fog-risk-to-active-fog path left to right. Same index always shows the same color. Thermodynamic risk with an observed-visibility density confirmation — not a copy of the visibility layer.<br><b>Units:</b> fog index 0-100 (display; derived, see derivation)<br><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NCEP HRRR 3 km analysis, hourly cycles: TMP+DPT+RH (2 m) + UGRD/VGRD (10 m) + VIS surface (derived fog-risk index with active-fog visibility gate)<br><b>Update:</b> Checked hourly; republishes only on a newer HRRR cycle.<br><b>Provenance:</b> <a href="https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl">https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl</a></p>]]></description>
